**Rolled:** Netlify for born-again minimalists. The first draw (Stripe × amateur YouTube influencers) was already in history, so one reroll.

**Product:** Shed, "Continuous un-deployment for people who sold the couch and have told you about it twice." Every merged pull request sends a courier to take one possession away, and rollbacks are not supported.
- **Features:** deploy previews of absence, atomic declutters, and edge functions at the front door that return 403 to gifted candles.
- **Pricing:** One Bowl ($0), The Monk ($49) and Nothing ($2,400).
- **Testimonials:** Dana Okafor, Pieter Vandermolen and Gil Hartmann.

**Design:** monastic gallery minimalism on washi paper with a celadon accent (Noto Serif JP / Noto Sans JP / IBM Plex Mono). Features hang as museum wall labels over empty plinths, and one large counter drops from 412 to 31. Style, palette and fonts all differ from the last 7 entries.
- The frontend-design skill lists a cream background with serif type and hairline rules as a common generated-page default. I kept it because it suits a minimalism brief and set it apart with the plinths, wall labels and counter. This is noted in the run log.

**Demo:** a deploy console listing 47 possessions with joy scores. You can filter by room, search and stage items, then press Deploy to stream a build log while the rows strike through and the count drops toward a target of 33. Protected items can't be staged, and Rollback is permanently refused with a message.

**Images:** all 4 slots used (1 wide hero photo of a near-empty room, 3 testimonial headshots). Each passed the visual check on the first attempt. The demo got none because a list-and-log screen doesn't need photos.

**Visual check:** 2 captures, 1 fix. The demo's side panel scrolled out of view on the long list, which hid the Deploy button, so I made it stay on screen on desktop. I re-ran metadata and validation afterwards and both passed. The landing page needed no changes.

**Recorded:** the gallery now has 121 slides with the new one first. `state/history.json` has the new entry appended, and the run summary is in `state/runs/2026-10-12.log`.

